(window.__D=window.__D||{})["data/ch08.json"]={"n": 8, "title": "The long 19th century (1800–1914)", "events": [{"id": "H5-023", "date": "1801", "date_text": "1801", "precision": "year", "title": "Russia annexes eastern Georgia; idle mines at Gümüşhane", "summary": "Russia annexed Kartli-Kakheti, bringing the Akhtala Greeks under the tsar and opening a Russian door for later Pontic emigrants. The same year an Ottoman document lists Koronixa and others among the idle mines of Gümüşhane.", "people": ["Alexander I"], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman document of 1801 (Karabıçak p. 130 n. 37)"], "sources_modern": ["Karabıçak 2021"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: general history (annexation of Kartli-Kakheti); Karabıçak 2021", "Ottoman state records: document of 1801 listing idle mines"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Tbilisi", "lat": 41.716, "lon": 44.783, "role": "annexed kingdom"}, {"name": "Koronixa (Görükse)", "lat": 40.5, "lon": 39.42, "role": "idle mine (coordinates approximate)"}]}, {"id": "C3-017", "date": "1811", "date_end": "1812", "date_text": "1811; famine and plague 1811–12", "precision": "year", "title": "Derebey coalition holds the coast; Koralla razed", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that in 1811, while the vali Hazinedarzade Süleyman Pasha was away at Poti, the derebeys of Gonia, Rize, Of and Sourmena joined those of Trebizond, Koralla, Tripolis and Kerasous and held the whole coast. The pasha returned by sea and razed Koralla, destroying its Greek community. Famine and plague followed in 1811–12, and many fled Trebizond for Vazelon.", "people": ["Hazinedarzade Süleyman Pasha"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.713–714"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Bryer (1969) treats the last derebey risings; Chrysanthos gives the Greek side and the destruction of Koralla.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, pp.713–714"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Koralla (Görele)", "lat": 41.0308, "lon": 39.0031, "role": "razed; Greek community destroyed"}, {"name": "Poti", "lat": 42.146, "lon": 41.672, "role": "where the vali was"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "derebeys"}, {"name": "Vazelon monastery", "lat": 40.7667, "lon": 39.5333, "role": "refuge from famine and plague"}]}, {"id": "H5-024", "date": "1814", "date_end": "1817", "date_text": "1814 - 26 October 1817", "precision": "year", "title": "Tuzcuoğlu Memiş Ağa's rising", "summary": "Memiş Ağa of the Tuzcuoğulları, bey of Gönye and warden of Batum, was condemned at the request of the governor of Trabzon, Hazinedarzade Süleyman Pasha, and rose in revolt. In summer 1816 his men held the coast from Hopa to Giresun, and on 18 August 1816 they took the port and then the fortress of Trabzon; Tirebolu, Tonya and the road to Gümüşhane followed. Government forces of about 30,000 pushed back. Of was besieged from May 1817, and Memiş was captured and executed on 26 October 1817.", "people": ["Tuzcuoğlu Memiş Ağa", "Hazinedarzade Süleyman Pasha", "Mahmud II"], "sources_primary": ["Cevdet Pasha, Tarih", "Ottoman archival orders"], "sources_modern": ["Aktepe 1953", "Bryer 1969 (Bedi Kartlisa 26)"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: archival orders", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Cevdet Pasha, Tarih (Ottoman official history, for dates); Aktepe 1953", "Neutral sources: Bryer 1969"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Rize", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 40.517, "role": "Tuzcuoğlu base"}, {"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "port and fortress taken 18 Aug 1816"}, {"name": "Hopa", "lat": 41.39, "lon": 41.43, "role": "eastern limit of rebel control"}, {"name": "Giresun (Kerasous)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "western limit of rebel control"}, {"name": "Of", "lat": 40.946, "lon": 40.268, "role": "besieged May-July 1817"}, {"name": "Gönye (Gonio)", "lat": 41.573, "lon": 41.573, "role": "Memiş Ağa's fortress"}]}, {"id": "H5-025", "date": "1817", "date_text": "From 1817", "precision": "year", "title": "Savvas Triantafyllidis heads the Phrontisterion", "summary": "Under Savvas Triantafyllidis, director from 1817, the Phrontisterion raised its standards. After 1839, and especially after 1856, teachers trained in Athens were allowed to teach there, among them Periklis Triantafyllidis, who taught philosophy and recorded the Pontic dialect.", "people": ["Savvas Triantafyllidis", "Periklis Triantafyllidis"], "sources_primary": ["P. Triantaphyllides, Οι φυγάδες (1870); Ποντικά (1866)"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: P. Triantaphyllides 1866/1870; Salvanou", "Neutral sources"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon (Phrontisterion)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "school"}]}, {"id": "C3-018", "date": "1821", "date_text": "1821", "precision": "year", "title": "Chrysanthos: Metropolitan Parthenios saved the Christians of Trebizond in 1821", "summary": "Chrysanthos says that in 1821 'τοὺς χριστιανοὺς Τραπεζοῦντος καὶ τῆς ὅλης ἐπαρχίας ἔσωσεν ἐκ βεβαίας σφαγῆς ὁ τότε ἀρχιερατεύων αὐτῆς Παρθένιος' (Metropolitan Parthenios saved the Christians of Trebizond and the whole diocese from certain massacre). He treats the protection given by the Satiroglou derebeys as a timariot's protection of his serfs.", "people": ["Parthenios (metropolitan of Trebizond)"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.714–715"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "Chrysanthos gives no details or sources.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, pp.714–715"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "metropolis"}]}, {"id": "C3-019", "date": "1826", "date_end": "1844", "date_text": "1826–1844; act of the administrators dated 1 December 1830", "precision": "year", "title": "The community's 'political fund' of Trebizond", "summary": "Chrysanthos describes the 'πολιτικὴ κάσσα' (political fund) of the Trebizond community, founded in 1826 as a graded tax on members under threat of excommunication. It was run by the political elder, secretaries, cashiers and key-holders (act of 1 December 1830), lasted until 1844, and its surplus went to build the Phrontisterion.", "sources_primary": ["Act of the fund's administrators, 1 December 1830 (fig. 109); Triantafyllidis, Οἱ Φυγάδες, as cited by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.680–682"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, pp.680–682"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "community fund"}]}, {"id": "H5-026", "date": "1829", "date_end": "1830", "date_text": "1828-1830 (Erzurum taken 27 June 1829 Old Style; Treaty of Adrianople 14 September 1829; withdrawal 1830)", "precision": "year", "title": "Paskevich takes Erzurum; Christians of the Erzurum region leave with the Russians", "summary": "In the war of 1828-29 Paskevich's army took Kars (1828), then Erzurum, and fought at Bayburt on the edge of Chaldia. When it withdrew in 1830 after the Treaty of Adrianople, Greeks of the Erzurum region who had sided with the Russians, or feared reprisals, left with it. Hassiotis puts the number at 42,000, under the label 'Eastern Anatolia (Erzerum)'; the Gümüşhane district was probably part of that movement but is not named in the table. They were settled in Tsalka and Trialeti, emptied districts of southern Georgia, where many of their descendants were Turkish-speaking 'Urum'. Bryer dates the failure of the Gümüşhane mines to these same years.", "people": ["Ivan Paskevich", "Nicholas I", "Mahmud II"], "sources_primary": ["Russian military reports; Treaty of Adrianople, art. 13 (right to emigrate)"], "sources_modern": ["Hassiotis 1997, via Pratsinakis 2013 (UvA PhD), ch. 3, Table 3.1, p. 46: 'Eastern Anatolia (Erzerum), after the 1828 Russo-Turkish War: 42,000'", "Xanthopoulou-Kyriakou 1991, Journal of Refugee Studies", "Tzedopoulos 2009, p. 172 (mines failed late 1820s, after Bryer)", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, pp. 26-27 (the 1829 invasion reached Kurum; emigration of Kurumlis to the Caucasus), p. 31 (families leave with Paskevich), p. 32 and p. 33 n. 43 (mines failed c. 1829)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Hassiotis' 42,000 covers 'Eastern Anatolia (Erzerum)', not the Pontus or Gümüşhane as such, and mixes Greek-speakers and Turkish-speaking Orthodox; some summaries give only '20% of the Greek population of the eastern Pontus'. Bryer (1983) says that the Russians briefly occupied Argyroupolis (Gümüşhane) in 1829 and that Christian families left with Paskevich's army to found Pontic villages in the Caucasus; since then, he writes, 'some thousands (some estimate 90.000)' Kromlides had gone to the Russian Caucasus, without saying who made the estimate.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Hassiotis 1997 (via Pratsinakis 2013); Xanthopoulou-Kyriakou 1991; Tzedopoulos 2009", "Neutral sources: Russian military reports; Treaty of Adrianople, art. 13", "Neutral sources: Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4 (international scholar, British; British consular archives)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kars", "lat": 40.602, "lon": 43.095, "role": "taken 1828"}, {"name": "Erzurum", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.267, "role": "taken 1829"}, {"name": "Bayburt", "lat": 40.255, "lon": 40.225, "role": "fighting 1829"}, {"name": "Gümüşhane", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "origin of emigrants"}, {"name": "Edirne (Adrianople)", "lat": 41.677, "lon": 26.556, "role": "treaty, 14 Sept 1829"}, {"name": "Tsalka", "lat": 41.596, "lon": 44.088, "role": "main settlement area"}]}, {"id": "H5-027", "date": "1829", "date_end": "1869", "date_text": "1829/30-1869 (rise; peak 1860); decline after 1869-1872", "precision": "decade", "title": "The Trebizond-Erzurum-Tabriz transit trade", "summary": "After the Treaty of Adrianople (1829), and the Anglo-Persian trade agreement of 1842, the Trebizond–Erzurum–Tabriz route reopened to Western trade: British manufactures for Persia came by steamer to Trebizond and went on by caravan to Tabriz, and Persian goods came back the same way. James Brant became the first British vice-consul at Trebizond in 1830, the Foreign Office aiming at 'the making of Trebizond a depot for the Persian trade'; steamships appeared on the south Black Sea coast from 1836, and Trebizond came to have regular lines to Feodosiya, Trieste and Southampton and up to a dozen consuls. The peak came in 1860, and by 1869 Trebizond carried about 40% of Persia's trade (Bryer). From 1869 the Suez Canal offered another route, and by 1872 Poti, with favourable Russian tariffs, had taken more than half of Trebizond's declining trade, though there were revivals, especially after 1883. A handful of Greek families with foreign connections became very rich, but the trade itself stayed largely in European hands (Bryer).", "people": ["James Brant (British consul)"], "sources_primary": ["British consular trade reports, Trebizond (Parliamentary Papers)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1980, XI ('The Tourkokratia in the Pontos', 1970), pp. 50–51: 'The Revival of the Trebizond-Tabriz Route, 1829-1869' (Adrianople; the Anglo-Persian agreement of 1842; steamship lines; peak in 1860; Suez 1869; Poti by 1872; revival after 1883)", "Bryer 1980, XII ('The Pontic revival and the new Greece', 1976), p. 180: reopening in 1830; by 1869 'Trebizond was carrying 40% of the trade of Persia'", "Bryer 1980, IV ('The Fate of George Komnenos', 1973), p. 340: 45,000 pack animals a year brought up to 40% of Persia's annual exports from Tabriz to the P. & O. steamers at Trebizond", "Özdiş, H., 'The consuls' (Trabzon), Black Sea and its port-cities 1774–1914 project (cities.blacksea.gr/en/trabzon/1-8-2): James Brant, first British vice-consul, from 1830", "Yılmaz, Ö. 2015, 'European navigation in the Black Sea at period of steamship', Karadeniz 25: 46–65 (the first steamship on the southern Black Sea coast in 1836) [Turkish; neutral fact]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The consulate (Brant, from 1830) and the first steamers on the southern coast (1836) are attested; which line first served Trebizond is not known.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Issawi 1970; British consular trade reports", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Özdiş (port-cities project); Yılmaz 2015"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "port"}, {"name": "Gümüşhane", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "caravan stage"}, {"name": "Bayburt", "lat": 40.255, "lon": 40.225, "role": "caravan stage"}, {"name": "Erzurum", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.267, "role": "caravan stage"}, {"name": "Tabriz", "lat": 38.08, "lon": 46.292, "role": "terminus"}, {"name": "Poti", "lat": 42.146, "lon": 41.672, "role": "rival port (more than half of Trebizond's trade by 1872, Bryer)"}]}, {"id": "C3-020", "date": "1831", "date_end": "1836", "date_text": "1831–1836; patriarchal letter of 4 January 1836", "precision": "year", "title": "Eyüpoğlu timariots expelled from Matsouka", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that from 1831 to 1836 the churches of Trebizond and Chaldia, the three monasteries and the rayah of Matsouka fought together against the Eyüpoğlu Muslim timariots and finally, through the Patriarchate, had them expelled from Matsouka (letter of 4 January 1836).", "people": ["Konstantios (metropolitan of Trebizond)"], "sources_primary": ["Patriarchal letter of 4 January 1836, as cited by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.618"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, p.618"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Matsouka (Maçka)", "lat": 40.8111, "lon": 39.6132, "role": "timariots expelled"}]}, {"id": "H5-028", "date": "1832", "date_end": "1834", "date_text": "September 1832 - December 1834", "precision": "year", "title": "The last Tuzcuoğlu rising and the end of the Pontic derebeys", "summary": "Abdülkadir and Tahir Tuzcuoğlu rose again, seizing Gönye in September 1832 and raising about 10,000 men. Governor Hazinedarzade Osman Pasha sent 8,000 men by land and 6,000 by sea, and in spring 1834 he besieged Of with 15,000. Abdülkadir was executed at Erzurum; his brothers were exiled to Ruse and Varna in December 1834. Osman Pasha governed Trabzon until his death in 1841-42, the last great figure of the derebey era, as Mahmud II's state took direct control.", "people": ["Tuzcuoğlu Abdülkadir", "Tuzcuoğlu Tahir", "Hazinedarzade Osman Pasha", "Mahmud II"], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman archival orders; Cevdet Pasha, Tarih"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1969", "BlackSea port-cities project, 'Hazinedarzade'"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Osman Pasha's governorship is dated from 1827 or from 13 July 1831 to his death in 1841 or 1842.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: archival orders", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Cevdet Pasha, Tarih (for dates)", "Neutral sources: Bryer 1969"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Gönye (Gonio)", "lat": 41.573, "lon": 41.573, "role": "seized Sept 1832"}, {"name": "Of", "lat": 40.946, "lon": 40.268, "role": "besieged spring 1834"}, {"name": "Erzurum", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.267, "role": "Abdülkadir executed"}, {"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "seat of Osman Pasha"}]}, {"id": "H5-029", "date": "1839-11-03", "date_text": "3 November 1839", "precision": "day", "title": "The Gülhane edict opens the Tanzimat", "summary": "The Hatt-ı Şerif of Gülhane promised all subjects security of life, honour and property, regular taxation and conscription. For the Pontic Greeks it opened the way to public churches, schools and confraternities. A penal code of 1843 declared Muslims and non-Muslims equal.", "people": ["Abdülmecid I", "Mustafa Reşid Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["Hatt-ı Şerif of Gülhane"], "sources_modern": ["Zürcher 2004", "Kuş 2018 (Belleten 82/294)"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Hatt-ı Şerif of Gülhane", "Neutral sources: Zürcher 2004", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Kuş 2018 (Belleten)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Istanbul, Gülhane", "lat": 41.013, "lon": 28.981, "role": "proclamation"}]}, {"id": "C3-021", "date": "1843", "date_end": "1845", "date_text": "Easter 1843 (demolitions); new building finished 1845", "precision": "year", "title": "Church of Christ and community school demolished at Easter; new Phrontisterion built", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that on Easter Day 1843 a Turkish crowd demolished the church of Christ in Trebizond and on Easter Monday the newly built community school, which 'ἐξελήφθη ὡς φρούριον' (was taken for a fortress) because it was higher than the firman allowed. Metropolitan Konstantios built the Phrontisterion anew on a conspicuous site by the shore; it was finished in 1845 and he paid most of its cost himself.", "people": ["Konstantios (metropolitan of Trebizond)"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.622; pp.715–716"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, p.622; pp.715–716"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "church of Christ and school demolished; new Phrontisterion"}]}, {"id": "H5-030", "date": "1844", "date_text": "1844", "precision": "year", "title": "Execution for apostasy abandoned", "summary": "Under pressure from the British ambassador Stratford Canning, the Porte undertook to stop executing converts from Islam who returned to Christianity. It was the precondition for crypto-Christians to declare themselves without risking death.", "people": ["Stratford Canning", "Abdülmecid I"], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman note of 1844 to the British embassy", "Canning's circular of [26 March] 1844 and G. A. Stevens's acknowledgement of 13 April 1844 (PRO FO 195/225, no. 27), quoted by Bryer 1983, p. 15 n. 4"], "sources_modern": ["Deringil 2000, 'There is no compulsion in religion', CSSH 42", "Tzedopoulos 2009, p. 165 n. 1", "Zürcher 2004, p. 61", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, p. 15 and n. 4 (G. A. Stevens, Acting Consul at Trebizond, 13 April 1844, acknowledging Stratford Canning's circular 'of the 26th Ultimo' on the Porte's engagement; PRO FO 195/225, no. 27)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Bryer (1983) says that a return to Christianity was punished with death 'until the Tanzimat of 1839', and names no such martyrs after 1839 (the last he names are Athanasios of Smyrna, 1819, and George of Ioannina, 1838); Canning's circular with the Porte's formal undertaking was acknowledged at the consulate at Trebizond on 13 April 1844.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Ottoman note of 1844 to the British embassy", "Neutral sources: Deringil 2000 (CSSH); Zürcher 2004", "Greek sources: Tzedopoulos 2009", "Neutral sources: Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4 (international scholar, British; British consular archives)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Istanbul", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "decision"}]}, {"id": "H5-031", "date": "1853", "date_end": "1856", "date_text": "1853-1856 (Kars falls 28 November 1855)", "precision": "year", "title": "The Crimean War on the Caucasus front", "summary": "The war brought fighting to the Batum-Kars front and Russian naval threats to the Pontic coast; Kars fell to the Russians in November 1855. The Kromni mines worked until 1854-55 and then failed for good. With them went the miners' privileges, which left the Kromlides facing conscription and taxes: the background to their declaration of 1857.", "people": ["Nikolay Muravyov-Karsky", "William Fenwick Williams"], "sources_primary": ["British and Russian military accounts"], "sources_modern": ["Tzedopoulos 2009, p. 173 n. 31", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, pp. 32-33"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Bryer (1983) dates the end differently: the silver mines of Argyroupolis were 'in their last gasp' by 1829 and closed soon after, by stages; the miners 'certainly became crypto-Christians about 1829', and those of 1857 inherited that status 'a generation or more after the mines had failed'. Tzedopoulos has the Kromni mines working until 1854–55; the two may speak of different mines, and the link from the failure of the mines to the declaration of 1857 is Tzedopoulos', not Bryer's.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: British and Russian military accounts", "Greek sources: Tzedopoulos 2009", "Neutral sources: Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4 (international scholar, British; British consular archives)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kars", "lat": 40.602, "lon": 43.095, "role": "besieged, fell Nov 1855"}, {"name": "Kromni (Kurum, today Yağmurdere)", "lat": 40.575, "lon": 39.862, "role": "mines closed 1854-55 (coordinates approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H5-034", "date": "1856", "date_end": "1882", "date_text": "1856-1882", "precision": "decade", "title": "Pontic emigration to the Russian Caucasus after the Crimean War", "summary": "Hassiotis counts about 53,000 Pontic Greeks leaving for the Caucasus between 1856 and 1882. They went by sea to Sukhum, Batum (after 1878), Novorossiysk and the Kuban, and overland to Georgia. Russian consuls encouraged them, and the Russian consulate promised help to Santa. Crypto-Christian and highland villages of Chaldia sent many.", "sources_primary": ["Russian resettlement records; consular reports"], "sources_modern": ["Hassiotis 1997, via Pratsinakis 2013, ch. 3, Table 3.1, p. 46: 'Pontos, after the Crimean War (1856-82): 53,000'", "Tzedopoulos 2009, p. 178 n. 48", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4: Document 2 (G. A. Stevens, 10 Nov 1857), pp. 42-44 and nn. 63-65; Document 1, p. 36 (Kurumlis taking Russian passports); p. 45 n. 68 (Russian passports and emigration, 1858-77)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The figure is a compiled estimate, and the numbers by destination are not given. A British consular despatch of 10 November 1857 reports that some 600 Greek families of Santa, mostly stonemasons and builders, were about to leave for Poti, where the Russian consul had promised work on the new harbour and land; their grievance was the seizure of their pasture by Muslim neighbours (Bryer 1983). Bryer warns that the Kromlides' list of 1857 counts only 615 houses at Santa, so the 600 'should be treated with caution'; he lists further consular reports: about 200 Russian passports in two or three months of 1858, 200 families tempted to the Crimea in 1861, and 50–200 families leaving Gümüşhane in 1864.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Hassiotis 1997 (via Pratsinakis 2013); Tzedopoulos 2009", "Neutral sources: Russian resettlement records; consular reports", "Neutral sources: Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4 (international scholar, British; British consular archives)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "port of departure"}, {"name": "Sukhum", "lat": 43.003, "lon": 41.016, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Novorossiysk", "lat": 44.723, "lon": 37.769, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Tsalka", "lat": 41.596, "lon": 44.088, "role": "destination"}]}, {"id": "H5-032", "date": "1856-02-18", "date_text": "18 February 1856", "precision": "day", "title": "The Islahat Fermanı (Hatt-ı Hümayun)", "summary": "The reform edict, issued just before the Treaty of Paris, confirmed equality and freedom of worship and allowed churches and schools to be built and repaired. A military-exemption tax (bedel-i askeri) for non-Muslims replaced the cizye. To the crypto-Christians of Chaldia it seemed to promise that they could declare their faith openly.", "people": ["Abdülmecid I"], "sources_primary": ["Islahat Fermanı, 18 February 1856"], "sources_modern": ["Tzedopoulos 2009, p. 172 n. 26, p. 178", "Kuş 2018", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, p. 15 (18 February 1856, ahead of the Treaty of Paris of 30 March) and p. 56 n. 78"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Islahat Fermanı", "Greek sources: Tzedopoulos 2009", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Kuş 2018", "Neutral sources: Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4 (international scholar, British; British consular archives)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Istanbul", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "proclamation"}]}, {"id": "H5-033", "date": "1856-05-14", "date_text": "14 May 1856", "precision": "day", "title": "The first Kromniot declares himself Orthodox", "summary": "Petros Sideropoulos, a crypto-Christian of Kromni, publicly declared himself Orthodox at Trebizond, the first such declaration after the edict. Chrysanthos (1933, pp. 716-717, after Ioannidis 1870, p. 144) gives the same date and calls him Petros Sava Sideropoulos of Kromni, a kavass of the Italian consulate known as Pehlil, who declared himself before the vali Hayreddin Pasha and the council, confessed three times, and was left free.", "people": ["Petros Sideropoulos"], "sources_primary": ["Greek community records as reported by Andreades"], "sources_modern": ["Topalidis, 'Crypto-Christians of the Trabzon region of Pontos' (trapezounta.com), after Bryer and Andreades 1995", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.716–717", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, pp. 15-16 ('Petros Savvas Sideropoulos', 14 May 1856, before Hayreddin pasha of Trebizond) and p. 56 n. 78 ('the first Kurumli')"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Greek community records via Andreades 1995; Topalidis", "Neutral sources: Bryer 1983, pp. 15-16, 56 n. 78", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "place of declaration"}]}, {"id": "H5-036", "date": "1857", "date_end": "1871", "date_text": "1857-1871", "precision": "year", "title": "The Ottoman reaction: the 'tenassur' category, conscription and harassment", "summary": "The Porte refused to treat the declarers simply as Christians. In the registers they were 'tenassur' (those who had become Christian), held liable for military service as former Muslims and also asked for Christian taxes. There were arrests and pressure in the villages: on 16/28 March 1859 about a hundred Kromlides, young and old, were jailed on the pretext of conscription and eleven were shipped to Constantinople; at a village near Gümüşhane men were jailed over Easter 1859, made to pay 2,000 piastres and then bastinadoed; a Kromli of Kerasous holding a Russian certificate was reportedly beaten and tortured in prison (British consular despatches, the Kromlides' petition to the consuls and the Russian consul's protest, printed by Bryer 1983, Documents 7-8a, pp. 52-58). Palgrave reported in 1867 that Constantinople, with the European ambassadors' agreement, had ruled that the Kromlides should serve but be exempt from the Imdadeeyah, the Christians' 20-piastre exemption tax; since each man had signed the village registers under a double name, Christian and Muslim, some were made to do both (Document 9, pp. 63, 65-66). The conscription law of 1871, which made service a Muslim duty, clashed with the tenassur status. Chrysanthos (1933, pp. 666, 670-671, 719-720) says the government treated them as 'Muslim apostates, Turks by nationality' and kept them liable for service; Metropolitan Konstantios II pressed for their exemption in 1898; Interior Ministry order no. 63 of 24 March 1326 (1910) let them register under Greek names only, and the metropolis issued a circular on 30 December 1910.", "people": ["Kromlides"], "sources_primary": ["British consular reports (Stevens, Biliotti, Palgrave) in FO 195 and FO 78", "British consular despatches printed by Bryer 1983: F. J. Stevens, 12 Feb and 5 May 1859 (FO 524/10), with the Russian consul's letter of 29 Jan/9 Feb 1859 and the Kromlides' petition of 23 March/4 April 1859 to the consuls; Palgrave, 17 April 1867 (FO 195/812)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4: Documents 7-8b, pp. 52-62 (the 1859 arrests; the Kromlides' petition to the consuls of 23 March/4 April 1859, pp. 56-58); Document 9, pp. 63-67 (Palgrave, 17 April 1867; the ruling and the double names, p. 66); p. 29 and n. 36 (1909-1910); p. 30 (Palgrave's three points); p. 33 ('obligation to neither')", "Tzedopoulos 2009, pp. 179-180", "Deringil 2012, Conversion and Apostasy in the Late Ottoman Empire", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.666; pp.670–671; pp.719–720"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Greek scholarship stresses persecution; Tzedopoulos stresses the double burden and the politics of sovereignty. Bryer (1983) prints the consular evidence of persecution in 1859, but stresses the Kromlides' own aim: after 1856 they still claimed to carry arms, to do no military service and to pay no poll tax, wanting 'obligation to neither'. A Turkish reading, of foreign instigation and the search for privileges, is given under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H5-T02). Chrysanthos dates the order of registration to 1910 but says elsewhere that the burden lasted 'until 1911'. Bryer (after Janin 1912 and Hasluck 1921) has the governor of Trebizond ask for a ruling on 24 February 1909 and convey the assurance that all Kromlides could declare their faith only on 29 December 1910, a day before the circular dated by Chrysanthos.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: British consular reports (Stevens, Biliotti, Palgrave); Bryer 1983; Deringil 2012", "Greek sources: Tzedopoulos 2009", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "provincial government"}, {"name": "Kromni (Kurum, today Yağmurdere)", "lat": 40.575, "lon": 39.862, "role": "affected villages (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H5-035", "date": "1857-07-15", "date_text": "15 July 1857 (the appeal sealed by 44 leaders); the list of villages is dated 30 August 1857", "precision": "day", "title": "The Kromni petition: an estimated 17,260 crypto-Christians ask to be registered as Christians", "summary": "The Kromlides petitioned the Porte and the ambassadors of the five major Christian Powers: on 15 July 1857 forty-four of their leaders sealed the appeal (Bryer 1983, p. 16), and their delegate, Mustafa son of Tursun, the Yazıcı (clerk), took four copies to Constantinople, for the British, Austrian, French and Russian representatives; the British acting consul, G. A. Stevens, says he had advised them to petition (Document 1, p. 35). When the embassy asked for details, Stevens sent a translation of the Kromlides' own list of villages, dated Trebizond, 30 August 1857, with his despatch of 31 October (pp. 35-41). Its totals line gives 11,151 houses: 1,907 Muslim, 3,452 Kromlides (crypto-Christian) and 5,792 Greek (open Christian). Its soul figures (Muslims 9,535; Kromlides 17,260; Greeks 28,960) are the houses ('families' in the totals lines) multiplied by five, an estimate, not a count; together they make an estimated 55,755 people (52% open Christians, 31% crypto-Christians, 17% Muslims). Stevens thought the list too low, as its compilers admitted (p. 36). Bryer's note speaks of 59 villages (55 rows, four of them pairs). Kromni, Santa and Stavri held more than 22% of the crypto-Christians (771 of the list's 3,452 Kromli houses). The Russian consulate's secretary helped draft the petitions (Ioannidis 1870), and a joint commission of embassy and Ottoman officials visited Trebizond. Chrysanthos (1933, pp. 717-719) prints the sealed letter of attorney of 16 July 1857 (fig. 110): representatives of crypto-Christian villages of the Trebizond and Chaldia dioceses (Kromni, Santa, Kovasa, Stavri, Mouzena, Galiana and others, with about 50 seals) met at Trebizond and appointed three attorneys to the Patriarchate and the ambassadors of England, France, Austria, Russia and Greece.", "people": ["Kromlides", "the British, Russian and French consuls at Trebizond", "George Alexander Stevens (British acting consul at Trebizond, 1841-58)", "Mustafa son of Tursun, the Yazıcı (the Kromlides' delegate to Constantinople)"], "sources_primary": ["The two 1857 lists and the petition (FO and Russian consular archives; Greek copies)", "G. A. Stevens, Acting Consul, to Viscount Stratford de Redcliffe, Trebizond, 31 Oct 1857, no. 46 (FO 524/10), with the translated list of 30 Aug 1857: Bryer 1983, Document 1, pp. 35-41"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4: p. 16 and n. 5 (44 leaders sealed the appeal of 15 July 1857 to the Porte and the ambassadors; 'about 17,260' Kurumlis); Doc. 1, pp. 35-36 (G. A. Stevens to Stratford de Redcliffe, 31 Oct 1857, FO 524/10) and pp. 37-41 (the list, translated 30 Aug 1857; totals p. 41); p. 37 n. 3 (59 villages; 18 of them named in the petition in Chrysanthos, p. 718); p. 30 n. 37 (reliability of the figures)", "Tzedopoulos 2009, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 16, pp. 169 ('some 17,000'), 173 n. 27 (Kromni, Santa and Stavri 'more than 22%'), 177 n. 43 (petition text in Chrysanthos, pp. 717-719), 176-179", "Bryer 2009 (paper of 2006), as summarised by Topalidis", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.717–719", "Ioannidis 1870, p. 144 (meeting of 150 at Theoskepastos, March 1857: oath, 18,700 piastres, delegates Sidiropoulos and Voskopoulos; reports drafted by Joseph Ricard of the Russian consulate) [Greek]", "Chrysanthos 1933, p. 717 (the representatives' meeting in Trebizond, 15 July 1857) [Greek]"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Some 17,000 (Tzedopoulos) or about 17,260 (Bryer 1983) is the list's 3,452 Kromli houses multiplied by five; Bryer prints no grand total, and 55,755 is the sum of the three estimated figures. Bryer warns that the list follows no Ottoman boundaries, covers only villages where crypto-Christians lived, probably undercounts the crypto-Christians, the open Christians more and the Muslims most, and leaves out wholly Muslim villages; but for the heartland 'almost every village seems to be included'. On motive, Bryer argues that the Chaldian miners' special status outlived the mines, which failed about 1829, leaving them neither officially Christian nor Muslim and wishing to owe 'obligation to neither'; Tzedopoulos sees several identities and the act of 1857 as a modern political choice backed by Russia. Chrysanthos' 'about 20,000' revealed and recognised is a round figure without basis or date. Ioannidis (1870) describes a meeting of 150 at the Theoskepastos in March 1857, with an oath, a collection of 18,700 piastres and reports drafted by Joseph Ricard of the Russian consulate; Chrysanthos dates the representatives' meeting in Trebizond to 15 July 1857. Ioannidis' delegate Nikolaos Tavropoulos of Poulantzaki and Chrysanthos' 'Tosunoğlu Mustafa Yazıcı, later Tavropoulos' are probably the same man under his Christian and Muslim names; that is an inference.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Bryer 1983 (British consular archives, FO 524/10; the Kromlides' list of 1857)", "Greek sources: the Greek petition (via Chrysanthos); Tzedopoulos 2009", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "petition presented"}, {"name": "Kromni (Kurum, today Yağmurdere)", "lat": 40.575, "lon": 39.862, "role": "home of the Kromlides (approximate)"}, {"name": "Santa (Dumanlı)", "lat": 40.68, "lon": 39.784, "role": "crypto-Christian centre"}, {"name": "Stavri (İstavri, today Uğurtaşı)", "lat": 40.582, "lon": 39.512, "role": "crypto-Christian centre"}]}, {"id": "H5-037", "date": "1859", "date_text": "1859", "precision": "year", "title": "About 8,000 Kromlides recognised as Orthodox", "summary": "According to Kallifron, about 8,000 of the declarers were recognised as Christians in 1859; the rest remained in limbo for decades. Kromni's population fell to about half its pre-collapse level by 1870 as families emigrated to Russia or moved to the coast.", "people": ["Kallifron (Greek writer)"], "sources_primary": ["Kallifron (19th-c. Greek account)"], "sources_modern": ["Tzedopoulos 2009, p. 180 n. 55 ('only 8,000 Kromlides' recognised in 1859, after Kallifron); p. 181 n. 65", "Ioannidis 1870, p. 145 ('about 20,000' came forward and were recognised) [Greek]", "Agtzidis 2005, p. 100 (24,000 recognised after the Crimean War, per the British consul; nn. 22–23) [Greek]", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, pp. 29, 41, 56-58, 65-66 (evidence against a recognition in 1859)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Kallifron is the only source for the 8,000. Chrysanthos' 'about 20,000' follows Ioannidis (1870), for whom it is the number who came forward and whom the government then recognised; Agtzidis (2005) gives 24,000 recognised after the Crimean War, after the British consul. Bryer (1983) has no recognition in 1859: the Kromlides' petition to the consuls of March–April 1859 still asks for their admission among the Christian subjects, Palgrave in 1867 describes a compromise ruling (military service, but no exemption tax), and Bryer puts the recognition of '12–30,000' in 1910. No British consular figure of 24,000 appears in the despatches Bryer prints, which give about 17,260 Kromlides (the list of 1857) and about 10,000 souls at Kromni (Palgrave 1867).", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Kallifron (19th-c. Greek account), via Tzedopoulos 2009"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kromni (Kurum, today Yağmurdere)", "lat": 40.575, "lon": 39.862, "role": "home district (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H5-038", "date": "1863-05", "date_end": "1867-09-07", "date_text": "May 1863 (patriarchal tomos creating the archbishopric) - 7 September 1867 (abolished; exarchates restored)", "precision": "month", "title": "Rhodopolis: an archbishopric made from the three monastic exarchates (1863-1867)", "summary": "A patriarchal tomos of May 1863 created the archbishopric (not yet a metropolis) of Rhodopolis from the villages that the monasteries of Soumela, Vazelon and Peristera had governed as exarchates, 'more than forty' villages between the dioceses of Trebizond and Chaldia; its archbishop was Gennadios. The monasteries and many villagers, Santa among them, refused it: in June 1865 the monasteries' supervision was moved to the metropolitan of Trebizond, patriarchal exarchs reported against the see, and it was abolished on 7 September 1867, the exarchates being restored. After a new inquiry (1874) the Patriarchate decided again in 1876 to keep the exarchates; Rhodopolis became a metropolis only in October 1902 (C3-030), one of the six Pontic metropolises of 1920. For the three exarchates without Santa, the Matzouka district, Bryer (1983) gives for 1857 ten villages with 11,160 open and crypto-Christians and 1,450 Muslims, from the Kromlides' list of 1857. The figures often quoted for 'Rhodopolis' (14,525 people: 53 percent open Christians, 37 percent crypto-Christians, 10 percent Muslims; Topalidis) are not printed by Bryer, but they are exactly what the same list gives for Santa and the three exarchates together: 2,905 houses at five a house.", "people": ["Gennadios, archbishop of Rhodopolis", "Konstantios, metropolitan of Trebizond", "Patriarch Sophronios III (letter of 1866)", "Patriarch Gregory VI (abolition, 1867)", "Patriarch Joachim II (decision of 1876)"], "sources_primary": ["Patriarchal tomos of May 1863, indiction 6 (Codex ΚΘ΄, pp. 151-152), quoted by Chrysanthos 1933, p. 686", "Patriarchal letters of 14 June 1865 and 27 June 1866, and the patriarchal and synodal letters of 1867 abolishing the archbishopric, printed by Kyriakidis 1898, pp. 208-211", "Kyriakidis 1898, p. 231: the Patriarchate's decision of 1876 to keep the exarchates, with Patriarch Joachim II's letters"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.634 (May 1863, 14 June 1865, 7 September 1867); pp.638–639; pp.686–687; p.667", "Kyriakidis 1898, Ἱστορία τῆς... μονῆς... Σουμελᾶ, pp. 206-235", "Ioannidis 1870, p. 245: Soumela 'stavropegial and an exarchate of the Patriarch', with more than 1,600 families", "Bryer 1980, study VI ('The Late Byzantine Monastery in Town and Countryside', 1979), p. 240 and n. 78", "Topalidis (trapezounta.com), 'per the 1857 petition' (figures not printed in Bryer 1983, but equal to the sum of its Document 1 rows for Santa and Bryer's areas 7-9, houses x 5)", "Bryer, A. 1983, 'The crypto-Christians of the Pontos and Consul William Gifford Palgrave of Trebizond', Deltio Kentrou Mikrasiatikon Spoudon 4: 13–68, doi 10.12681/deltiokms.35: n. 37 (pp. 30–31, estimates for the exarchates and Matzouka in 1520, 1857, 1868, 1912, 1920) and Document 1 (pp. 37-41, totals p. 41: the Kurumli village list, translated 30 Aug. 1857 and sent with Stevens's despatch of 31 Oct. 1857, FO 524/10, after the petition of 15 July 1857)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Bryer (1983) gives further counts for the district: 12,066 Christians and 18,460 Muslims in 1868 (Palgrave, the Muslims 'wildly overestimated') and 16,525 Christians and 5,335 Muslims in 1920 (Zerzelides). His own figures for 1857 cannot be rebuilt exactly from the village rows of the list, which give 2,000 Christian houses and 2,290 houses in all; the reason for the difference is not known.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: patriarchal acts as printed by Chrysanthos 1933 and Kyriakidis 1898; Ioannidis 1870; Topalidis", "Neutral sources: Bryer 1979/1980"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Maçka (Matsouka)", "lat": 40.813, "lon": 39.612, "role": "core of the metropolis"}, {"name": "Soumela monastery", "lat": 40.69, "lon": 39.658, "role": "former exarchate"}, {"name": "Vazelon monastery", "lat": 40.74, "lon": 39.59, "role": "former exarchate (approximate)"}, {"name": "Peristera monastery", "lat": 40.884, "lon": 39.8, "role": "former exarchate (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H5-039", "date": "1864", "date_text": "Spring-autumn 1864", "precision": "month", "title": "Circassian and Ubykh refugees die in thousands at Trabzon and Samsun", "summary": "After Russia's final conquest of the western Caucasus, Circassians, Ubykhs and Abkhaz were shipped across the Black Sea. On 12 April 1864 about 25,000 were already at Trebizond, and by mid-1864 some 40,000 were in two camps there. Vice-Consul Stevens reported about 300 deaths a day at the Saradere and Akçakale camps in June and put mortality at 50% in September. Dr Barozzi found 70,000-80,000 at Samsun in May; a private letter spoke of 500-700 deaths a day. Survivors were settled inland, many in the Samsun, Amasya, Tokat and Sivas hinterlands, which changed the ethnic map of the western Pontus.", "people": ["Vice-Consul Stevens", "Dr Barozzi", "Consul Dickson", "Sir Henry Bulwer"], "sources_primary": ["FO 97/424; FO 881/1259; FO 881/3065 (Stevens reports, 17 Feb, 18 June, 24 Sept 1864)", "Barozzi's report, The Times 13 June 1864", "Papers respecting the Settlement of Circassian Emigrants in Turkey (1864)"], "sources_modern": ["Rosser-Owen 2007 (SOAS MA)", "Richmond 2013, The Circassian Genocide"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Fauvel's figure of over 300,000 arrivals in the empire between November 1863 and August 1864, two-thirds of them dead, may be inflated, and the daily death rates are snapshots. Whether to call the expulsion a genocide is disputed: Richmond and Circassian organisations use the word, the Russian state rejects it, and Georgia recognised it in 2011.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: British consular and sanitary reports (FO 97/424; FO 881; Stevens; Barozzi in The Times)", "Neutral sources: Rosser-Owen 2007; Richmond 2013"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon (camps at Saradere and Akçakale)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "landing and camps (camp sites not located)"}, {"name": "Samsun (camps at Irmak and Dervend; Kumcuğaz)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "landing and camps"}, {"name": "Sukhum", "lat": 43.003, "lon": 41.016, "role": "embarkation"}]}, {"id": "H5-040", "date": "1867", "date_end": "1873", "date_text": "1867-1873", "precision": "year", "title": "Consul Palgrave reports on the crypto-Christians", "summary": "William Gifford Palgrave, British consul at Trebizond (1867-73), reported on the Kromlides in a confidential report of 17 April 1867, one of many answers to a French, Russian and British circular of that year on the status of Christians. He counted at 'Kroom' 'about two thousand hearths, or ten thousand souls', long publicly Muslim and privately Christian, who after the Crimean War had declared themselves Christian, many taking Russian papers, and then refused both military service and the Imdadeeyah, the Christians' exemption tax; Constantinople ruled that they should serve but be exempt from the tax. Bryer notes that Palgrave saw three anomalies: they carried arms like Muslims, did no military service like Christians, but unlike Christians paid no poll tax in lieu of it. The explanation from the miners' old status (harac excused for mining or charcoal service) is Bryer's, not Palgrave's (pp. 32-33). The phrase 'neither Mussulmans nor Christians' is G. A. Stevens's, in 1857 (Document 1, p. 35). Bryer's 1983 study prints the report as Document 9, with the Stevens brothers' despatches of 1857-59 (Documents 1-8).", "people": ["William Gifford Palgrave"], "sources_primary": ["Palgrave's consular dispatches (FO 195, FO 78)", "Palgrave, report of 17 April 1867 (enclosure 1 to despatch no. 6, FO 195/812), printed by Bryer 1983 as Document 9, pp. 63-67; his 'Report on the Provinces of Trebizond, Sivas, Kastemouni, and part of Angora', Accounts and Papers 59 (1868-69), 335-400 (cited by Bryer, p. 25 n. 28)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4: pp. 25-30 (Palgrave and the Kurumlis; his three points, p. 30), pp. 63-67 (Document 9, his report of 17 April 1867, FO 195/812), p. 67 n. 87 (consul 1867-73)", "Tzedopoulos 2009, p. 169 n. 15, p. 180 n. 57"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Tzedopoulos doubts that the miners were exempt from the poll tax. Bryer treats Palgrave as a biased witness, a 'mishellene' who despised Greeks and regarded the Kromlides as apostates, 'a demonstrably careless witness' who in his parliamentary report derived them from Xenophon's Ten Thousand; he also shows that Palgrave had himself lived as the Jesuit Père Michel Sohail and changed his religion up to six times.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Palgrave's consular dispatches (FO 195, FO 78); Bryer 1983", "Greek sources: Tzedopoulos 2009"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "British consulate"}]}, {"id": "H5-041", "date": "1870", "date_text": "C. 1870", "precision": "year", "title": "The Gümüşhane mines almost all abandoned", "summary": "The Trabzon provincial yearbook of 1870 describes nearly all the Gümüşhane mines as abandoned and flooded, too deep to be measured. A kaymakam reported that a third of the people left their lands soon after the mines closed. The end of mining pushed Chaldiots to Russia, to the coast and to the plains of Samsun.", "sources_primary": ["Trabzon Vilayet Salnamesi 1870 (1287 H)"], "sources_modern": ["Karabıçak 2021, pp. 122, 127, 132 n. 48 (citing Özcan San)", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, p. 32"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Bryer (1983) has the mines closed 'soon after' 1829, by stages, and the miners moving to Russia, to new mines at Ak Dag (1832) and to the alum mines north of Şebinkarahisar, taking their special status with them; the yearbook of 1870 describes the end.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Trabzon Vilayet Salnamesi 1870; a kaymakam's report", "Neutral sources: Karabıçak 2021", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Özcan San (as cited by Karabıçak)", "Neutral sources: Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4 (international scholar, British; British consular archives)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Gümüşhane", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "mining district"}]}, {"id": "C3-026", "date": "1870", "date_end": "1874", "date_text": "1870 (Sourmena); 1874 (Cheriana, Agridi)", "precision": "year", "title": "Violence against Christians in Sourmena and Chaldia", "summary": "Chrysanthos records crimes in 1870 'ἰδίᾳ ἐν τῇ περιφερείᾳ Σουρμένων' (especially in the Sourmena district): seizure of property, robbery of churches and abduction of girls, against which Konstantios obtained two high orders. In 1874 he obtained the expulsion of two Ottomans, Kadir and Ahmed, from Ulu-Cheriana in Chaldia, and there was a chapel dispute at Agridi.", "people": ["Konstantios (metropolitan of Trebizond)"], "sources_primary": ["Patriarchal letters of 1870 and 1874, as cited by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.618–620"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, pp.618–620"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sourmena (Sürmene)", "lat": 40.91046, "lon": 40.11405, "role": "violence of 1870"}, {"name": "Argyroupolis (Gümüşhane, old town)", "lat": 40.4598, "lon": 39.4778, "role": "Chaldia: Ulu-Cheriana, 1874"}]}, {"id": "C3-027", "date": "1872", "date_text": "1872", "precision": "year", "title": "Greek–Armenian clash in Trebizond", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that in 1872 a rupture between Greeks and Armenians followed the abduction of an Orthodox girl by Armenians, and remarks that the Armenians 'συχνὰ ἐχρησιμοποιοῦντο ὑπὸ τῶν Τούρκων ὡς ὄργανα κατὰ τῶν ὀρθοδόξων' (were often used by the Turks as tools against the Orthodox).", "sources_primary": ["Patriarchal correspondence, as cited by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.620–621"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Chrysanthos' comment on the Armenians is partisan.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, pp.620–621"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "clash"}]}, {"id": "H5-042", "date": "1877", "date_end": "1878", "date_text": "1877-1878 (Kars taken 18 November 1877; Treaty of Berlin 13 July 1878)", "precision": "year", "title": "Russia takes Kars, Ardahan and Batum", "summary": "Russia annexed Kars, Ardahan and Batum. Many Muslims left Kars oblast in 1879-82, and Christians took their place: Armenians, Russians and Greeks. Greeks from Gümüşhane, Trebizond and the older Georgian colonies founded Greek villages and towns in Kars oblast (77 Greek villages of Kars and Ardahan, according to Agtzidis). Muslim Laz, Ajarians and Georgians from the ceded lands moved the other way into Ottoman territory, many settling around Ordu, Samsun and Sinop.", "people": ["Mikhail Loris-Melikov"], "sources_primary": ["Treaty of San Stefano; Treaty of Berlin; Russian Kars oblast records"], "sources_modern": ["Agtzidis, in A. Diamantopoulos (ed.) 2001, Η ιστορία των Ελλήνων του Καυκάσου στο Καρς και στο Κιλκίς, p. 18: 'τα 77 ελληνικά χωριά του Καρς και του Αρνταχάν' [Greek]; the same sentence in Agtzidis 2001, Παρευξείνιος Διασπορά, 2nd ed.", "Bryer 1980; Mikhailidis & Athanasiadis 2007"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The numbers of Greek settlers from the Ottoman Pontus and from Georgia are not separated. The 77 villages are Agtzidis' figure.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: treaties of San Stefano and Berlin; Kars oblast records", "Greek sources: Mikhailidis & Athanasiadis 2007", "Greek sources: Agtzidis 2001 (77 villages)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kars", "lat": 40.602, "lon": 43.095, "role": "annexed"}, {"name": "Batumi", "lat": 41.642, "lon": 41.634, "role": "annexed"}, {"name": "Gümüşhane", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "origin of settlers"}, {"name": "Ordu (Kotyora)", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "Muslim refugee settlement"}]}, {"id": "C3-028", "date": "1878", "date_end": "1880", "date_text": "Soumela plundered 1877–78 (arrests 5 May 1878); patriarchal letters of 3 May, 9 May and 3 June 1880", "precision": "year", "title": "After the war of 1877–78: Soumela plundered, Laz and Circassian immigrants, Christian emigration from Kerasous", "summary": "Chrysanthos records that fourteen Turks who had sacked Soumela were arrested on 5 May 1878 after the Patriarchate intervened. With Batum annexed by Russia, 'οἱ ἐν τῇ περιφερείᾳ Βατοὺμ κατοικοῦντες Λαζοὶ καὶ Κιρκάσιοι Μουσουλμάνοι μετηνάστευσαν εἰς τὰς παραλίους ἐπαρχίας τοῦ Πόντου, συχνὰ ἐπιτιθέμενοι … κατὰ τῶν ἐκεῖ χριστιανῶν' (the Laz and Circassian Muslims of the Batum region moved to the Pontic coastal districts, often attacking the Christians there), especially around Kerasous and Kotyora. Many Christians of the Kerasous district were forced to emigrate. In 1879–80 the Russian Church detached Batum 'αὐθαιρέτως καὶ ἀντικανονικῶς' (arbitrarily and uncanonically) from the diocese.", "sources_primary": ["Patriarchal letters of 5 May 1878 and 3 May, 9 May and 3 June 1880, as cited by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.640; pp.642–643; p.688"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "No figures for immigrants or emigrants are given.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, p.640; pp.642–643; p.688"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Soumela monastery", "lat": 40.69, "lon": 39.6583, "role": "plundered 1877–78"}, {"name": "Batum", "lat": 41.6417, "lon": 41.6367, "role": "annexed by Russia; church detached"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.3874, "role": "Laz immigrants settled; Christian emigration"}, {"name": "Kotyora (Ordu)", "lat": 40.97782, "lon": 37.89047, "role": "Laz immigrants settled"}]}, {"id": "H5-043", "date": "1879", "date_text": "1879", "precision": "year", "title": "Parcharides counts the Greek-speaking Muslims of Of", "summary": "Ioannis Parcharides' statistics of Of give 10,000-12,000 families, of whom 8,000-10,000 spoke Greek, but only 192 families were Christian. It is the best 19th-century measure of the Greek-speaking Muslim population. Parcharides also argued that conversion began well before the late 17th century, under pressure from bandits and Anatolian settlers.", "people": ["Ioannis Parcharides"], "sources_primary": ["Parcharides 1879, pp. 224-232 (Greek)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1968, p. 110", "Poutouridou 1997, p. 57 n. 41", "Topalidis (trapezounta.com)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "A Greek contemporary estimate, not a census.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Parcharides 1879; Poutouridou 1997; Topalidis", "Neutral sources: Bryer 1968"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Of", "lat": 40.946, "lon": 40.268, "role": "district counted"}]}, {"id": "H5-044", "date": "1883", "date_end": "1914", "date_text": "1883 (Tobacco Régie) - 1914", "precision": "decade", "title": "Tobacco and hazelnuts: the boom of the western Pontic plains", "summary": "Samsun's rise began before the Régie: the chaussée to Amasya and the spread of Bafra tobacco made it, by the 1860s, the port of the Constantinople–Baghdad route with international tobacco markets; it overtook Trebizond in population in the 1870s; Bryer puts its first peak at about 70,000 in 1900 (Bryer 1980), Bryer and Winfield at over 40,000 by 1910 (1985, p. 94). After the Ottoman Public Debt handed the tobacco monopoly to the Régie (1883), Samsun-Bafra tobacco remained a major export. Greeks rose to as much as 40% of Samsun's people, and Greek and Armenian control of local business and of the tobacco market became 'almost exclusive', though this growth owed more to immigration from Ionia and Constantinople than to Pontic Hellenism (Bryer). Hazelnuts did the same for Kerasous, Ordu and Tirebolu. The two trades drew highland Greeks from Chaldia, Kromni, Santa and the Trebizond hinterland down to the Samsun, Bafra and Çarşamba plains and the Ordu-Fatsa-Ünye coast.", "sources_primary": ["Trabzon Vilayet Salnameleri (1879, 1896, 1901)", "British consular reports", "Cuinet 1890-95"], "sources_modern": ["Quataert 1983, Social Disintegration and Popular Resistance in the Ottoman Empire (on the Régie)", "Bruneau (on Pontic migrations)", "Yolalıcı 1998 (Canik)", "Bryer 1980, XI ('The Tourkokratia in the Pontos', 1970), pp. 51–52: 'The Rise of Amisos (Samsun)'"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Trabzon salnames (1879, 1896, 1901)", "Neutral sources: British consular reports; Cuinet; Quataert 1983; Bruneau", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Yolalıcı 1998"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "tobacco port"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.907, "role": "tobacco"}, {"name": "Çarşamba", "lat": 41.199, "lon": 36.723, "role": "tobacco and maize plain"}, {"name": "Giresun (Kerasous)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "hazelnuts"}, {"name": "Ordu (Kotyora)", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "hazelnuts"}, {"name": "Tirebolu", "lat": 41.006, "lon": 38.814, "role": "hazelnuts"}]}, {"id": "H5-045", "date": "1886", "date_text": "1886 (seminary at Merzifon since 1862)", "precision": "year", "title": "Anatolia College at Merzifon", "summary": "The American Board moved its seminary from Bebek to Merzifon (Marsovan) in 1862 and opened Anatolia College there in 1886, with Charles Tracy as president. Its students were mostly Greek and Armenian, with Greek, Armenian and American teachers, and a girls' school was added in 1893. It became a meeting point of the two Christian communities, and a target in 1893, 1895, 1915 and 1921.", "people": ["Charles Tracy"], "sources_primary": ["Annual reports of the American Board (ABCFM)"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: ABCFM annual reports"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Merzifon (Marsovan)", "lat": 40.873, "lon": 35.463, "role": "college"}]}, {"id": "H5-046", "date": "1892", "date_text": "1890-1895 (Cuinet, La Turquie d'Asie I)", "precision": "decade", "title": "Cuinet's statistics of the vilayet of Trebizond", "summary": "Vital Cuinet's survey gives the vilayet's population by kaza and religion, from 'les dernières données' of the early 1890s. Cuinet prints a total of 1,047,700 inhabitants (including Şiran and Kelkit) and 806,700 Muslims (691,700, plus 55,000 Laz and 60,000 Circassians). A sandjak column reading 91,000 / 77,000 / 16,000 / 9,000 sums to 193,000; Beylerian, reading Cuinet (I, pp. 10–11), gives the vilayet's Greeks as 193,000 and its Armenians as 47,200. It is the most detailed pre-1914 breakdown by kaza.", "people": ["Vital Cuinet"], "sources_primary": ["Cuinet, La Turquie d'Asie, vol. I (Paris 1892), Trébizonde chapter (archive.org item VitalCuinet.LaTurquieDAiseParisE.Leroux1892.Volume1, djvu text)"], "sources_modern": ["Beylerian, A., Revue d'histoire arménienne contemporaine 1, table quoting Cuinet I (1891), pp. 10–11 (imprescriptible.fr/rhac/tome1/pdf/trebizonde.pdf) [Armenian]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Cuinet relied on Ottoman figures, which undercounted Christians.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Cuinet 1892 (French author)", "Ottoman state records: the Ottoman data Cuinet used", "Armenian sources: Beylerian (RHAC 1), reading Cuinet"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "vilayet capital"}]}, {"id": "H5-047", "date": "1895-10-08", "date_end": "1895-11-15", "date_text": "8 October 1895 (Trebizond); late October (Gümüşhane, Bayburt); 15 November (Merzifon, Amasya)", "precision": "day", "title": "The Hamidian massacres reach Trebizond", "summary": "On 2 October 1895 two Armenians wounded Bahri Pasha, a former governor, and Hamdi Pasha at Trebizond. On 8 October a massacre of Armenians followed in the streets and houses of the city, and Laz bands then plundered Armenian villages nearby. Contemporary reports put the dead in the city at several hundred: 'four hundred' in a period narrative, 'over five hundred killed' in the American minister's telegram of 16 October, and 'nine hundred … said to have been massacred' in a press cable. Estimates for the vilayet run to 1,000-2,000. Massacres followed at Gümüşhane, Bayburt, Merzifon and Amasya. Western accounts say the Greeks were not targeted.", "people": ["Abdülhamid II", "Bahri Pasha", "Hamdi Pasha", "Alphonse Cillière (French consul)", "H.Z. Longworth (British consul)"], "sources_primary": ["Terrell to Olney, 16 Dec 1895 (FRUS 1895 II, doc. 683; no Trebizond figure)", "Press cable, Manawatu Herald, 31 Oct 1895 ('nine hundred … said to have been massacred')", "Period narrative reprinted by globalsecurity.org ('four hundred')", "Cillière, 1895, massacres d'Arméniens (ed. Dédéyan, Mouradian, Ternon, 2010)", "British consular reports (Longworth), Blue Book Turkey No. 2 (1896) (not consulted)", "Ottoman report of the officials and notables of Trebizond, via Mavroyeni Bey, 27 Dec 1895 (FRUS 1895 II, doc. 701): its account is under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H5-T03)", "Terrell to Olney, telegram, 16 Oct 1895 (FRUS 1895 II, doc. 599): 'over five hundred killed' at Trebizond 'last week' (history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1895p2/d599)"], "sources_modern": ["Kévorkian 2011; Suny 2015"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The '8,000' in a modern notice of the French consul Cillière's report is almost certainly an error, since the same notice gives 8,000 as the Armenian population.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: FRUS 1895 II, doc. 683; press cable (Manawatu Herald, 31 Oct 1895); period narrative; Cillière; Suny 2015", "Armenian sources: Kévorkian 2011"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "massacre 8 Oct 1895"}, {"name": "Gümüşhane", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "massacre (late Oct 1895)"}, {"name": "Bayburt", "lat": 40.255, "lon": 40.225, "role": "massacre (late Oct 1895)"}, {"name": "Merzifon", "lat": 40.873, "lon": 35.463, "role": "massacre (Nov 1895)"}, {"name": "Amasya", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "massacre (Nov 1895)"}]}, {"id": "H5-048", "date": "1897", "date_text": "28 January 1897 (Russian imperial census)", "precision": "day", "title": "32,593 Greek speakers in Russian Kars oblast", "summary": "The first Russian census counted 290,654 people in Kars oblast, of whom 32,593 (11.2%) were Greeks by native language (the census counted by native language). Turkish-speaking Orthodox (Urum) were probably counted as Turkish speakers, so the Greek total is a minimum.", "sources_primary": ["Первая всеобщая перепись населения Российской империи 1897 г., Карсская область"], "sources_modern": ["Agtzidis (ed.) 2001 (Kars volume), p. 22 (the 1897 census, counting by mother tongue, missed the 'Turkish-speaking and Armenian-speaking Greek populations' of Transcaucasia) [Greek]"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The census counted by mother tongue, so the Turkish-speaking Orthodox (Urum) are hidden among the Turkish-speakers.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Russian imperial census of 1897 (via the Demoscope compilation)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kars", "lat": 40.602, "lon": 43.095, "role": "oblast capital"}]}, {"id": "H5-049", "date": "1899", "date_end": "1906", "date_text": "1899-1906", "precision": "year", "title": "Pressure on the Stavriotes of Akdağ Madeni", "summary": "From 1899 the Stavriote crypto-Christians, Chaldiot miners settled around Akdağ Madeni who had not joined the 1857 movement, came under growing pressure. Their leaders were exiled in 1905, some dying in exile, and in 1906 they appealed to Greece and the Patriarch. Ottoman documents name leaders such as Küpcüoğlu of 'the Istavri movement'.", "people": ["Küpcüoğlu (leader of the Istavri movement)"], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman archival documents (BOA); Patriarchal publications of 1909"], "sources_modern": ["Tzedopoulos 2009, pp. 182-183", "Türkyılmaz 2009 (UCLA PhD), via Ottoman History Podcast no. 104", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, p. 32; p. 33 n. 43"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Bryer (1983) has Kromlides moving to 'new mines at Ak Dag in 1832' and says that the crypto-Christian grievances after 1857 came largely from such mining communities; Ak Dag is not in the list of 1857, and the events of 1899–1906 are not in his article.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: BOA documents (exile of the leaders, 1905)", "Greek sources: Patriarchal publications of 1909; Tzedopoulos 2009", "Neutral sources: Türkyılmaz 2009 (UCLA PhD)", "Neutral sources: Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4 (international scholar, British; British consular archives)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Akdağ Madeni", "lat": 39.66, "lon": 35.884, "role": "Stavriote centre"}]}, {"id": "H5-050", "date": "1902", "date_text": "1902", "precision": "year", "title": "The new building of the Phrontisterion", "summary": "The Phrontisterion moved into a new three-storey building with 36 classrooms above the seafront, near the church of St Gregory of Nyssa and the Armenian cathedral. It was the most imposing Greek monument of late Ottoman Trebizond and became a symbol of Pontic Greek education, with about 1,047 Greek schools in the region by about 1910. It closed in November 1921, and today the building houses the Kanuni Anadolu Lisesi. Chrysanthos (1933, pp. 663-665) records the community schism of 1893-1897, the decision to rebuild after the reconciliation of 1897, funds collected in Russia, the firman of 2 March 1899, and the consecration on 14 and 16 September 1902 before the vali Kadri Bey and the Muslim, Armenian and Persian representatives.", "sources_primary": ["School yearbooks (Πρόγραμμα του Φροντιστηρίου)"], "sources_modern": ["Pavlidis, Αρχείον Πόντου suppl. 24", "Koromila (schools c. 1910, from project notes)", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.663–665"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: school yearbooks; Pavlidis; Koromila (schools c. 1910)", "Neutral sources", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Phrontisterion building (Kanuni Anadolu Lisesi), Trabzon", "lat": 41.006, "lon": 39.733, "role": "school (coordinates approximate)"}]}, {"id": "C3-030", "date": "1902-10", "date_text": "Patriarchal and synodal tomos of October 1902", "precision": "month", "title": "Rhodopolis re-established as a metropolis", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that a patriarchal and synodal tomos of October 1902 re-established Rhodopolis as a METROPOLIS from the three exarchates of Soumela, Vazelon and Peristereota. Its first metropolitan, Gervasios Sarasitis, 'δεινῶς ἐπολεμεῖτο' (was fiercely opposed) by the monasteries and by villagers they incited. In 1895 Santa still belonged to the exarchate of Soumela.", "people": ["Gervasios Sarasitis"], "sources_primary": ["Patriarchal and synodal tomos of October 1902 (Codex 978, pp.21–23), as cited by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.667; p.687"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "This corrects the older view that the metropolis of Rhodopolis dates from 1863 (H5-038).", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, p.667; p.687"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Soumela monastery", "lat": 40.69, "lon": 39.6583, "role": "former exarchate"}, {"name": "Vazelon monastery", "lat": 40.7667, "lon": 39.5333, "role": "former exarchate"}, {"name": "Peristereota monastery (Kuştul)", "lat": 40.7911, "lon": 39.72, "role": "former exarchate"}, {"name": "Santa (Dumanlı)", "lat": 40.66888, "lon": 39.80164, "role": "in the Soumela exarchate until 1902"}]}, {"id": "C3-031", "date": "1904-11-19", "date_text": "High order of 19 November 1904", "precision": "day", "title": "High order against Laz brigands in the Rize district", "summary": "Chrysanthos records a high order of 19 November 1904 against the Laz Muslim brigands of the Rize district, whose violence against Christians had begun after the death of the vali Kadri Bey.", "people": ["Kadri Bey"], "sources_primary": ["High order of 19 November 1904, as cited by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.666"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, p.666"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Rizaion (Rize)", "lat": 41.0208, "lon": 40.5219, "role": "brigandage against Christians"}]}, {"id": "H6-001", "date": "1908-07-24", "date_text": "24 July 1908 (elections Nov–Dec 1908)", "precision": "day", "title": "The Young Turk revolution reaches Pontus", "summary": "Sultan Abdülhamid II restores the 1876 constitution under pressure from the Committee of Union and Progress (CUP). Greeks, Armenians and Muslims celebrate together in Trebizond and Samsun, and Pontic Greeks vote in the parliamentary elections of late 1908: Matthaios Kofidis was elected for Trebizond, and again in 1912 and 1914, while at Amisos (Samsun) the Greek candidate was not elected (Boura 1983). Optimism fades quickly: the CUP's Ottomanism soon turns into a nationalist economic and military programme that the Greeks of Pontus see as aimed at them.", "people": ["Abdülhamid II", "Matthaios Kofidis"], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman parliamentary records (Meclis-i Mebusan Zabıt Ceridesi), 1908–1912", "Ehali (Samsun), list of 4 Oct 1921, as reprinted in Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914–1922 (Athens 1922), sect. VI, leaf 36 (archive.org scan; no printed page numbers, cited by section and scan leaf) ('Mathieu Coffidés, ex-député de Trébizonde dans le parlement Ottoman')", "Locum tenens of the Ecumenical Patriarchate to the League of Nations, 13/26 Nov 1921, in Le Martyre du Pont-Euxin et l'opinion publique internationale (Geneva 1922; Greek advocacy compilation, preface by A. Wuarin), p. 44 ('the former Member for Trebizond')"], "sources_modern": ["Hanioğlu, Preparation for a Revolution (2001)", "Kieser, Talaat Pasha (2018)", "Gingeras, Fall of the Sultanate (2016)", "Boura, K. 1983, 'Οι βουλευτικές εκλογές στην Οθωμανική αυτοκρατορία: οι Έλληνες βουλευτές 1908–1918', Deltio Kentrou Mikrasiatikon Spoudon 4: 69–85, doi 10.12681/deltiokms.36, p. 72 nn. 17–18, p. 79"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Boura (1983) shows that Kofidis was deputy for Trebizond in the three parliaments of 1908–1912, 1912–1914 and 1914–1918, and that no Greek was elected at Amisos in 1908; no other Greek deputy for a Pontic constituency is named. Kofidis' time as deputy is also stated by the Black Book and the Martyre.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Ottoman parliamentary records", "Neutral sources: Hanioğlu 2001; Kieser 2018; Gingeras 2016", "Greek sources: Boura 1983"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trebizond (Trapezous, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "joint celebrations; seat of the vilayet"}, {"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "joint celebrations; Canik sancak"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "parliament"}]}, {"id": "H5-051", "date": "1909", "date_end": "1910", "date_text": "1909-1910", "precision": "year", "title": "The constitutional era: Kromlides and Stavriotes registered under Christian names", "summary": "After the 1908 revolution the Kromlides petitioned the deputy Bousios in the Ottoman parliament (1909). The Patriarchate published documents blaming Abdülhamid's regime. In 1910 both Kromlides and Stavriotes were allowed to register under Christian names only, although during the First World War the Stavriotes were refused recognition and drafted into Muslim units.", "people": ["Georgios Bousios (deputy)"], "sources_primary": ["Εκκλησιαστική Αλήθεια 1909-1911"], "sources_modern": ["Tzedopoulos 2009, pp. 183-184, 197-198", "Andreades 1995, p. 84 (declarations went on to 1910)", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.666; pp.670–671; pp.719–720", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, p. 29 and n. 36"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Bryer (1983, after Janin 1912 and Hasluck 1921) puts the recognition of '12–30,000' Kromlides as Christians in 1910, 'two years after the last Ottoman constitution', through Russian and French rather than British pressure: the governor of Trebizond asked for a ruling on 24 February 1909 and conveyed the assurance only on 29 December 1910. Chrysanthos dates the Interior Ministry order 24 March 1326 (1910) and the metropolis' circular 30 December 1910. Bryer does not deal with the Stavriotes.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Εκκλησιαστική Αλήθεια 1909-1911; Tzedopoulos 2009; Andreades 1995", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933", "Neutral sources: Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4 (international scholar, British; after Janin 1912 and Hasluck 1921)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Istanbul (Ottoman parliament)", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "petition"}, {"name": "Akdağ Madeni", "lat": 39.66, "lon": 35.884, "role": "Stavriotes"}, {"name": "Kromni (Kurum, today Yağmurdere)", "lat": 40.575, "lon": 39.862, "role": "Kromlides (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H6-002", "date": "1909", "date_end": "1914", "date_text": "1909–1911 and 1913–1914", "precision": "year", "title": "Boycotts of Greek businesses", "summary": "A Muslim boycott of Greek merchants begins after the Cretan crisis of 1909–10 and is revived, now organised by the CUP, after the Balkan Wars. Its aim was a 'national economy' in Muslim hands. It hit the Aegean and Constantinople hardest; in Pontus it was weaker but present in the ports. At Trebizond, Chrysanthos says, the CUP ordered a boycott of the Pontic Greeks in early 1914, but the Turkish merchants themselves foiled it for fear of reprisals (Chrysanthos 1933, pp. 744-745). On 5 July 1914, with war with Greece feared, a circular to the provinces, Trebizond and Canik among them, ordered the boycott against the Greeks lifted while negotiations went on, and those who kept it up punished; Akçam reads this as a show for foreign opinion by a government that had organised the boycott (Akçam 2012, pp. 83-86). In wartime, a Greek report from Kerasous of 1917 says that delegates of the Committee told the Muslim villages that the Sultan had freed them from their debts to Christians and forbade paying them, or paying rent to Christian landowners; since the war began, it says, hardly any Turk had paid (Brown & Ion 1918, p. 17).", "sources_primary": ["Patriarchate complaints of 1913–14 (in Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914–1918, 1919)", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.744–745 (account of a participant)", "[Ottoman document] Interior Ministry circular of 5 July 1914 to Edirne, Adana, Aydın, Bursa and Trebizond and to Izmit, Bolu, Menteşe, Teke, Canik, Çatalca, Karesi and Kale-i Sultaniye: lift the boycott against the Greeks during the negotiations, warn and punish those who keep it up, BOA DH.ŞFR 42/199 (Akçam 2012, pp. 85-86)", "[Greek consular report, Kerasous, 1917] Greek Legation (Ministerial Archives no. 4901), in Brown & Ion 1918, p. 17"], "sources_modern": ["Bjørnlund, JGR 10:1 (2008), 41–58", "Akçam, The Young Turks' Crime against Humanity (Princeton UP, 2012), pp. 83-86 (the boycott of 1913-14 organised and overseen by the CUP; the circular of 5 July 1914 ordering it lifted, BOA DH.ŞFR 42/199, sent also to Trebizond and Canik) [Turkish-born scholar abroad]", "Hofmann, Abriss des Pontosgenozids (AGA)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Chrysanthos, a party to events, is the only source for the failure of the boycott of 1914 at Trebizond.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Patriarchate complaints of 1913-14", "Neutral sources: Bjørnlund 2008; Akçam 2012; Hofmann", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Smyrna (İzmir)", "lat": 38.419, "lon": 27.129, "role": "main boycott centre"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "boycott centre"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "Pontic port affected"}, {"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "Pontic port affected"}]}, {"id": "H6-003", "date": "1909-07", "date_text": "July 1909 (service made compulsory for all Ottoman subjects); October 1909 (first call-up irrespective of religion)", "precision": "month", "title": "Non-Muslims made liable for military service", "summary": "In July 1909 military service was made compulsory for all Ottoman subjects, ending the practice by which Christians paid the bedel instead of serving; in October 1909 conscripts were called up irrespective of religion for the first time (Zürcher). Many young Christian men, especially Greeks, chose to leave the country or to acquire a foreign passport; from the Pontus they left for Russia, the Caucasus and the United States, along the routes of the 19th-century emigration, in numbers not yet known. The law is the legal root of the 1914 labour battalions.", "sources_primary": ["Ottoman military service law of 1909 (Düstur)"], "sources_modern": ["Zürcher, E. J. 1999, 'The Ottoman conscription system in theory and practice, 1844–1918', in Zürcher (ed.), Arming the State, p. 89 ('In July 1909 military service was made compulsory for all Ottoman subjects'; first call-up irrespective of religion in October 1909; Christians, 'especially Greeks', leaving or taking foreign passports); earlier version in International Review of Social History 43 (1998): 437–449", "Xanthopoulou-Kyriakou 1991 (Pontic emigration to Russia)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Zürcher gives no numbers for the emigration, and its scale and destinations from the Pontus are not known.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: military service law of 1909", "Neutral sources: Zürcher 1998", "Greek sources: Xanthopoulou-Kyriakou 1991"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "emigration port"}, {"name": "Batumi", "lat": 41.642, "lon": 41.634, "role": "emigration destination"}, {"name": "Novorossiysk", "lat": 44.723, "lon": 37.769, "role": "emigration destination"}]}, {"id": "H5-052", "date": "1912", "date_text": "1910-1912 (Greek Patriarchal-consular census); figures published 1918", "precision": "year", "title": "Greek counts of the Orthodox of Pontus: the 1910-12 census, and Soteriades 1918", "summary": "The Greek (Patriarchal-consular) census of 1910-12 counted 400,586 Orthodox in the six Pontic metropolises (Kitromilides & Alexandris). By vilayet the same series gives 298,183 for Trabzon with Canik and 74,632 for Sivas. A different set of figures, 353,533 for the vilayet of Trebizond and 99,376 for Sivas, comes from Soteriades (1918), a work of advocacy published for the peace conference that claims a Patriarchal basis; the same table quotes an 'Ottoman official' figure of 351,104 for Trebizond in 1910. The Greek counts covered every Rum Orthodox, Greek- or Turkish-speaking.", "sources_primary": ["Greek Patriarchal-consular census of 1910-12, as tabulated by Kitromilides & Alexandris 1984-85", "Soteriades 1918 (Greek advocacy for the peace conference)"], "sources_modern": ["Kitromilides & Alexandris 1984-85, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 5, Table III, p. 27 (400,586)", "G0 vilayet table (298,183; 74,632), after en.wikipedia 'Greek genocide'", "Pentzopoulos 1962, p. 29 (via pontosworld), for the Soteriades figures"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "For the province of Trebizond with Canik the comparable range is 260,313 (Ottoman census of 1914) to 298,183 (Greek census of 1910–12), with 353,533 as an advocacy figure. Soteriades' 'Ottoman 1910' figure does not match the census of 1914.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Greek Patriarchal-consular census 1910-12 (Kitromilides & Alexandris); Soteriades 1918; Pentzopoulos 1962"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "vilayet"}, {"name": "Sivas", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.017, "role": "vilayet"}]}, {"id": "H6-004", "date": "1912-10-18", "date_end": "1913-08-10", "date_text": "18 Oct 1912 – Aug 1913", "precision": "day", "title": "The Balkan Wars", "summary": "The Ottomans lose almost all their European lands; Greece takes Macedonia, Epirus and the north Aegean islands. Some 350,000–400,000 Balkan Muslims flee to Anatolia (Pezo 2017), and CUP hostility to the empire's Christians hardens; the CUP seizes power in the coup of 23 January 1913. Pontic Greek men are conscripted for the first time in large numbers.", "people": ["Enver Pasha", "Talat Pasha", "Eleftherios Venizelos"], "sources_modern": ["Pezo, in The Balkan Wars from Contemporary Perception to Historic Memory (2017)", "Gingeras 2016"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Muslim refugee numbers vary widely: 350,000–400,000 (Pezo) against much higher figures in some Turkish and advocacy works (e.g. 900,000 'expelled or killed' in Hofmann's essay).", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Pezo 2017; Gingeras 2016; Hofmann"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Thessaloniki", "lat": 40.64, "lon": 22.944, "role": "taken by Greece, Nov 1912"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "CUP coup, 23 Jan 1913"}]}, {"id": "C3-033", "date": "1913", "date_end": "1914", "date_text": "Census of the metropolis, 1913–1914", "precision": "year", "title": "The metropolis' census of 1913–14: 45,765 souls present", "summary": "Chrysanthos prints the census 'συμφώνως πρὸς τὴν ἐν ἔτει 1913–1914 ὑπὸ τῆς μητροπ. Τραπεζοῦντος γενομένην στατιστικὴν ἀπογραφήν'. The city of Trebizond had 6,720 families: 3,500 Muslim, 2,500 Greek, 600 Armenian, 60 Persian and 60 foreign; the Greeks were in nine parishes with 13 churches and 12 schools. The diocese (77 settlements, from Tripolis to Rize, including eight Christian villages of Of) had 7,841 families, 96 churches and 89 schools (95 in the summary table), and 45,765 souls present (22,111 men, 23,654 women), of whom 45,275 Ottoman, 173 Hellenic, 308 Russian and 9 Persian subjects; 90 priests, 157 teachers and 5,540 pupils. A footnote adds 4,640 souls abroad, for 50,405.", "sources_primary": ["Census of the metropolis of Trebizond, 1913–1914, as printed by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.791–796"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "A church count by the metropolis, to be set beside the Greek census of 1910–12 and the Ottoman figures.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, pp.791–796"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "2,500 Greek families of 6,720"}, {"name": "Rizaion (Rize)", "lat": 41.0208, "lon": 40.5219, "role": "310 families"}, {"name": "Of", "lat": 40.946, "lon": 40.27, "role": "eight Christian villages"}, {"name": "Tripolis (Tirebolu)", "lat": 41.00735, "lon": 38.81452, "role": "western limit"}]}, {"id": "C3-034", "date": "1913", "date_end": "1914", "date_text": "Committee from 1913; contract with a Belgian company in 1914", "precision": "year", "title": "Joint Greek, Turkish, Armenian and Persian water committee of Trebizond", "summary": "Chrysanthos says that in 1913 a committee for the water supply of Trebizond, of Greeks, Turks, Armenians and Persians and chaired alternately by the metropolitan and the mufti, raised 'ἱκαναὶ δεκάδες χιλιάδων χρυσῶν λιρῶν' (many tens of thousands of gold pounds) from an import surtax and in 1914 signed a contract with a Belgian company; the war stopped the work.", "people": ["Chrysanthos"], "sources_primary": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.751–752 (account of a participant)"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, pp.751–752"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "water supply project"}]}, {"id": "C3-032", "date": "1913-10-12", "date_text": "12 October 1913", "precision": "day", "title": "Kerasous detached from Trebizond and given to Chaldia; the diocese's limits", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that on 12 October 1913 Kerasous was detached from Trebizond and attached to Chaldia, so that the diocese's western limit became Tripolis. 'In our days' the diocese ran along the coast from Tripolis to Batum (detached by the Russian Church in 1879–80) and inland to Kiourtouni, Dikaisimon (Tzevizlik), Olassa, Santa and Baiburt; in the northeast it had absorbed the sees of Rizaion, Ischopolis and Lazia, extinguished in the mid-17th century.", "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.687–688", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, p. 33 n. 43; p. 54 n. 76"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Bryer (1983) mentions Chaldia's claim, just before the First World War, to a 'corridor' to the sea at Bulancak between the dioceses of Neocaesarea and Trebizond, shown on the map of the Union Micrasiatique de Smyrne (1919), and explains it by Chaldian Kromlides settled on that coast; he dates the transfer of Kerasous to Trebizond to 1698, as Chrysanthos does (C3-014).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, pp.687–688", "Neutral sources: Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4 (international scholar, British; British consular archives)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.3874, "role": "transferred to Chaldia"}, {"name": "Tripolis (Tirebolu)", "lat": 41.00735, "lon": 38.81452, "role": "new western limit"}, {"name": "Batum", "lat": 41.6417, "lon": 41.6367, "role": "eastern limit until 1879–80"}, {"name": "Kiourtouni (Kürtün)", "lat": 40.6517, "lon": 38.3718, "role": "southern limit"}, {"name": "Matsouka (Maçka)", "lat": 40.8111, "lon": 39.6132, "role": "Dikaisimon (Tzevizlik), southern limit"}, {"name": "Olassa (Bahçekaya)", "lat": 40.8664, "lon": 39.7065, "role": "southern limit"}, {"name": "Santa (Dumanlı)", "lat": 40.66888, "lon": 39.80164, "role": "southern limit"}, {"name": "Baiburt (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.256313, "lon": 40.222887, "role": "southern limit"}]}, {"id": "H5-053", "date": "1914", "date_text": "1914 (Ottoman census, published figures)", "precision": "year", "title": "The Ottoman census of 1914", "summary": "The Ottoman census gives the vilayet of Trebizond, with Canik, 1,516,249 people. Of these, 1,187,078 were Muslims, 260,313 Greek Orthodox and 66,218 Armenians. By sancak the Greek Orthodox were 116,357 in Trabzon, 98,739 in Canik, 43,495 in Gümüşhane and 1,722 in Lazistan. Sivas vilayet, which includes Amasya, Tokat and Şebinkarahisar, had 75,324 Greek Orthodox, and Kastamonu, with Sinop, 20,958.", "sources_primary": ["Ottoman census 1914 (1330 H), published tables"], "sources_modern": ["Karpat 1985, Ottoman Population 1830-1914, pp. 180-190"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The Ottoman census undercounted non-Muslims (Shaw 1978; Kévorkian 2011). Canik was a separate (müstakil) sancak in 1914; the table adds it in.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Ottoman census of 1914 (published tables)", "Neutral sources: Karpat 1985"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "vilayet"}, {"name": "Samsun (Canik)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "sancak"}, {"name": "Gümüşhane", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "sancak"}, {"name": "Rize (Lazistan)", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 40.517, "role": "sancak"}, {"name": "Sivas", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.017, "role": "vilayet"}]}], "turkish": [{"id": "H5-T02", "date": "1856", "date_end": "1910", "date_text": "1856-1910", "precision": "decade", "title": "Turkish scholarship on the crypto-Christian declarations: foreign instigation and the search for privileges", "summary": "Turkish state-institution scholarship (Kuş 2018, in Belleten, the journal of the Turkish Historical Society; the study 'Trabzon vilayetinde iki din taşıyanlar') presents the Kromlides and Stavriotes who declared themselves Christian after 1856 as people encouraged by foreign powers, above all Russia, and moved by the wish to escape conscription and to gain privileges.", "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "petition presented"}, {"name": "Kromni (Kurum, today Yağmurdere)", "lat": 40.575, "lon": 39.862, "role": "home of the Kromlides (approximate)"}], "people": ["Kromlides", "Stavriotes"], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman documents as printed by Kuş 2018"], "sources_modern": ["Kuş 2018, Belleten 82/294", "'Trabzon vilayetinde iki din taşıyanlar'"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The Ottoman documents these works print are valuable; their interpretation is the Turkish reading.", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Kuş 2018 (Belleten, TTK); Trabzon vilayetinde iki din taşıyanlar"], "main": false, "counter_evidence": "Partly supported by Bryer (1983, British, from the British consular archives), who agrees that worldly motives and Russian backing played a part: by declaring themselves Christian, 'Kurumlis could not only evade military service, but seek Russian protection to avoid any other kind of obligation to the Ottoman state'. Palgrave in 1867 blamed Russian influence; Bryer traces their status to the Chaldian miners' exemptions after the mines failed about 1829; and the recognition of 1910 came through Russian and French pressure. The British acting consul Stevens says that he himself advised them to petition. Neither Bryer nor Tzedopoulos calls the declarations insincere: Bryer says that the concealment of faith was real ('there is no doubt that this was done') and their sufferings 'undoubted', while denying that they were long-enduring martyrs. Tzedopoulos reads the declarations as a modern political act by people of several negotiated identities, and Deringil's verdict is that 'what was at issue was not religion at all, but sovereignty'."}, {"id": "H5-T03", "date": "1895-10-08", "date_text": "8 October 1895 (report forwarded 27 December 1895)", "precision": "day", "title": "The Ottoman report on the Trebizond events of October 1895", "summary": "A report of the officials and notables of Trebizond, forwarded by the Ottoman minister in Washington, Mavroyeni Bey, on 27 December 1895, blames the violence of 8 October on Armenian gunfire, names no Armenian death toll, and says that 'the Greeks … had joined the brawl'.", "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "massacre 8 Oct 1895"}], "people": ["Mavroyeni Bey"], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman report of the officials and notables of Trebizond, via Mavroyeni Bey to Olney, 27 Dec 1895 (FRUS 1895 II, doc. 701)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "An official report written to answer Western accusations; the American diplomatic papers (Foreign Relations of the United States) print it in full.", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Ottoman official report (its account of blame and of the Greeks)"], "main": false, "counter_evidence": "Contemporary non-Ottoman reports describe a massacre of Armenians: 'four hundred' (a period narrative) to 'nine hundred … said to have been massacred' (press cable, Manawatu Herald, 31 Oct 1895); the French consul Cillière also reported it, and the vilayet toll is estimated at 1,000-2,000. Western accounts say the Greeks were not attacked; no non-Ottoman source known to us says that Greeks took part. The wounding of Bahri Pasha and Hamdi Pasha by two Armenians on 2 October is told in the chapter (H5-047)."}], "synthesis": "## The end of the derebeys\n\nThe 19th century opened with the coast still in the hands of local lords. In 1811, while the governor, Hazinedarzade Süleyman Pasha, was away at Poti, the derebeys of the eastern valleys joined those of Trebizond, Koralla, Tripolis and Kerasous and held the whole coast; the pasha came back by sea and razed Koralla (Görele), destroying its Greek community, and famine and plague followed (Chrysanthos). In 1814 Tuzcuoğlu Memiş Ağa, lord of Gönye and warden of Batum, rose in revolt; in the summer of 1816 his men held the coast from Hopa to Giresun, and on 18 August they took the port and the fortress of Trabzon. Government forces of about 30,000 pushed them back, Of was besieged from May 1817, and Memiş was captured and executed on 26 October 1817. A last rising of his kinsmen in 1832–34 ended when Hazinedarzade Osman Pasha besieged Of with 15,000 men. Osman Pasha governed Trabzon until his death in 1841 or 1842, the last great figure of the derebey age, as Mahmud II's state took direct control. For the Christians these decades meant insecurity and forced levies; their direct effect on the Greeks is thinly recorded.\n\n## Wars and emigration\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch08_emigration.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch08_emigration.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1280\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the eastern Black Sea showing Pontic Greek emigration to Tsalka, Kars and the Caucasus coast, the Circassians shipped to Trabzon and Samsun in 1864, the Muslims from Batum, and the Russian gains of 1878.\"></a><figcaption>Wars and emigration: the Pontic Greeks who went to Russian lands from 1829, the Circassians, Ubykhs and Abkhaz shipped across in 1864, the Muslims who left the Batum region, and the land Russia took in 1878. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\nEvery Russo-Turkish war emptied part of the Pontic highlands. In 1828–29 Paskevich's army took Kars and Erzurum and fought at Bayburt, on the edge of Chaldia. When the Russians withdrew in 1830, Christians who had sided with them or feared reprisals left with them: Hassiotis (1997) counts about 42,000, under the heading \"Eastern Anatolia (Erzerum)\", a figure that probably includes people from the Gümüşhane district but does not name it. They were settled in Tsalka and Trialeti in Georgia, many of them Turkish-speaking Orthodox, the \"Urum\". The Crimean War brought fighting on the Batum and Kars fronts, and Kars fell to the Russians in November 1855; Hassiotis counts about 53,000 Pontic emigrants to the Caucasus between 1856 and 1882, by sea to Sukhum, Novorossiysk and the Kuban and overland to Georgia. After the war of 1877–78 Russia annexed Kars, Ardahan and Batum, and Greek villages grew up in Kars province: the Russian census of 1897 counted 32,593 people of Greek mother tongue there, 11 percent of the population, a minimum, since the Urum were counted as Turkish speakers. Muslims moved the other way: Laz, Ajarians and Georgians from the Batum region settled on the Pontic coast, many round Ordu and Samsun, and Chrysanthos writes that they often attacked the Christians there, so that many Christians of the Kerasous district left.\n\n## The crypto-Christians come out\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch08_plains.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch08_plains.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1080\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map from Bafra to Bayburt showing the crypto-Christian centres of Santa, Kromni and Stavri, the highlanders moving down to the coastal plains, and the caravan road to Persia.\"></a><figcaption>The crypto-Christians come out and the highlanders move down: the crypto-Christian centres, the moves to the coast and to the tobacco plains of Samsun and Bafra, and the caravan road to Persia. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\nThe reforms of the Tanzimat made it possible for the crypto-Christians to declare themselves. The Gülhane edict of 1839 promised security and equality; in 1844, under pressure from the British ambassador Stratford Canning, the Porte undertook to stop executing converts from Islam who returned to Christianity; and the edict of 18 February 1856 confirmed freedom of worship. On 14 May 1856 Petros Sideropoulos of Kromni declared himself Orthodox at Trebizond, and on 15 July 1857 forty-four of the Kromlides' leaders sealed a petition to the Porte and the ambassadors of the Christian powers. The British acting consul, Stevens, says that he had advised them to petition; according to Ioannidis (1870), a secretary of the Russian consulate helped draft it. The list that went with it, printed by Bryer (1983), gives 11,151 houses in 59 villages: 1,907 Muslim, 3,452 crypto-Christian and 5,792 Greek. Its figures for souls are the houses multiplied by five, an estimate of about 55,800 people, of whom about 17,260 were crypto-Christians. Kromni, Santa and Stavri held more than a fifth of them (Tzedopoulos).\n\nThe Porte refused to treat them simply as Christians. It created a special category in the registers, *tenassur*, \"those who became Christian\", which made them liable to conscription as former Muslims while also charging them the Christians' tax. British consular despatches record arrests and beatings in 1859, when about a hundred Kromlides were jailed on the pretext of conscription. How many were recognised is uncertain: Kallifron says about 8,000 in 1859 (as cited by Tzedopoulos); Ioannidis (1870), followed by Chrysanthos, about 20,000; and Agtzidis (2005), after the British consul, 24,000. Between 17,000 and 20,000 is the best supported figure. The British consul Palgrave, in 1867, counted about two thousand hearths at Kromni and noted that the Kromlides still carried arms like Muslims and refused both military service and the Christians' exemption tax; Bryer warns that Palgrave despised the Greeks and was a careless witness. The rest waited. The conscription law of 1871 sharpened the conflict; from 1899 the Stavriotes round Akdağ Madeni came under pressure, and their leaders were exiled in 1905; only in 1910, after the constitution, could the Kromlides and Stavriotes register under Christian names. Deringil's verdict, quoted by Tzedopoulos, is that \"what was at issue was not religion at all, but sovereignty\".\n\n## Trade, steam, tobacco and hazelnuts\n\nFrom the 1830s Trebizond was the sea gate for British goods bound for Persia. They went by caravan through Gümüşhane, Bayburt and Erzurum to Tabriz, and Persian goods came back the same way; consulates and steamer lines followed. The route declined after the opening of the Suez Canal (1869), the Poti–Tiflis railway (1872) and the Batum–Baku line (1883) (Bryer).\n\nThe growth moved west. Samsun, joined to Amasya by a new road and to the world by Bafra tobacco, overtook Trebizond in population in the 1870s. Bryer puts its first peak at about 70,000 people in 1900 (Bryer 1980); Bryer and Winfield give over 40,000 by 1910 (1985, p. 94). After the Ottoman Public Debt handed the tobacco monopoly to the Régie in 1883, the tobacco of Samsun, Bafra and Çarşamba remained a great export, and hazelnuts did the same for Kerasous, Ordu and Tirebolu. Greek growers, brokers and merchant houses were prominent in both trades, though Bryer notes that much of Samsun's Greek growth came from immigrants from Ionia and Constantinople rather than from the Pontus itself.\n\n## Down to the plains\n\nThe closing of the mines, insecurity in the mountains and the new cash crops drew highland Greeks downhill. Bryer dates the end of the Gümüşhane silver mines to about 1829, and those of Kromni lasted to 1854–55; by 1870 the provincial yearbook found nearly all the mines flooded and abandoned. A kaymakam reported that a third of Gümüşhane's people left once the mines closed. Families from Chaldia, Kromni, Santa and the hinterland of Trebizond settled the plains of Samsun, Bafra and Çarşamba, the coast from Ordu to Fatsa and Ünye, and the interior of the diocese of Amaseia. This is why the metropolis of Amaseia, by then seated at Samsun, had 400 communities in 1922, many of them founded in the 19th century.\n\n## Church and schools\n\nThe community of Trebizond ran its own affairs through its metropolitan, its nine parishes and its elders. In 1826 it set up a \"political fund\", a graded tax on its members, whose surplus went to build the Phrontisterion. On Easter Day 1843 a Turkish crowd demolished the church of Christ in Trebizond and, the next day, the new community school, which was taken for a fortress because it was higher than the firman allowed; Metropolitan Konstantios built the Phrontisterion anew on the shore, paying most of the cost himself (Chrysanthos). Under Savvas Triantafyllidis, director from 1817, and later with teachers trained in Athens, among them Periklis Triantafyllidis, who recorded the Pontic dialect, the school raised its standards. Its new building of 1902, three storeys with 36 classrooms above the sea, now the Kanuni Anadolu Lisesi, crowned a network of about 1,047 Greek schools in the region by about 1910 (Koromila). In 1863 the Patriarchate made the villages of the three monasteries of Matzouka an archbishopric of Rhodopolis; it was abolished in 1867, and refounded as a metropolis only in October 1902, against the opposition of the monasteries.\n\nThe American Board moved its seminary to Merzifon in 1862 and opened Anatolia College there in 1886. Its students were mostly Greeks and Armenians, side by side, and it was attacked in 1893, 1895, 1915 and 1921.\n\n## The neighbours\n\nThe Armenians lived in Trebizond, Samsun, Ordu, Merzifon, Amasya and the Hemshin highlands; the Ottoman census of 1914 counted 66,218 in the province of Trebizond. Relations with the Greeks were not always easy: Chrysanthos records a quarrel in 1872 after the abduction of an Orthodox girl. On 2 October 1895 two Armenians wounded Bahri Pasha and Hamdi Pasha at Trebizond, and on 8 October a massacre of Armenians followed in the streets and houses of the city. Contemporary reports put the dead in the city at several hundred: \"four hundred\" in one account, \"over five hundred\" in the American minister's telegram of 16 October, nine hundred in a press cable; estimates for the province run to 1,000–2,000. Laz bands then plundered the Armenian villages nearby, and massacres followed at Gümüşhane, Bayburt, Merzifon and Amasya. Western accounts say the Greeks were not attacked.\n\nIn 1864, after Russia's final conquest of the western Caucasus, Circassians, Ubykhs and Abkhaz were shipped across the Black Sea. By the middle of the year some 40,000 were in two camps at Trabzon, where Vice-Consul Stevens reported about 300 deaths a day in June and put mortality at half by September; at Samsun Dr Barozzi found 70,000–80,000 in May. The survivors were settled inland, round Samsun, Amasya, Tokat and Sivas, in new Muslim villages beside Greek ones. Kurds lived on the southern edge, round Kelkit, Şiran and Sivas; the Turkmen and Chepni had held the hinterland of Giresun and Ordu since the 14th century; and the Laz and the Hemshin of the eastern coast are described in the previous chapter. Laz brigands of the Rize district were active again after 1900, and a high order of 1904 was issued against them.\n\n## The Greeks of the Pontus about 1910\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch08_metropolises.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch08_metropolises.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1120\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Map of the Pontus from Bafra to Rize with a dot for each Greek Orthodox place in the atlas gazetteer, coloured by metropolis, and squares for the villages of Greek-speaking Muslims.\"></a><figcaption>The Greek Orthodox places in the atlas gazetteer, coloured by metropolis, with the villages of Greek-speaking Muslims. Each metropolis is labelled with the number of its places and the number that have coordinates and appear on the map. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\n| Source | Area | Greek Orthodox | Kind of source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Cuinet, 1892 | province of Trebizond, with Şiran and Kelkit | about 193,000, of a total of 1,047,700 (806,700 Muslims) | a French author using Ottoman data |\n| Greek census, 1910–12 | the six Pontic metropolises | 400,586 | Patriarchal and consular count (Kitromilides & Alexandris 1984–85) |\n| Greek census, 1910–12 | province of Trebizond with Canik; province of Sivas | 298,183; 74,632 | the same |\n| Soteriades, 1918 | province of Trebizond; province of Sivas | 353,533; 99,376 | Greek advocacy for the peace conference |\n| Metropolis of Trebizond, 1913–14 | the diocese, Tripolis to Rize | 45,765 present, 50,405 with those abroad | the church's own count (Chrysanthos) |\n| Ottoman census, 1914 | province of Trebizond with Canik (Trabzon 116,357; Canik 98,739; Gümüşhane 43,495; Lazistan 1,722) | 260,313 | Ottoman, as published by Karpat; undercounts Christians |\n| Ottoman census, 1914 | province of Sivas; province of Kastamonu | 75,324; 20,958 | the same |\n\nFor the province of Trebizond with Canik the counts run from 260,313 (Ottoman, 1914) to 298,183 (Greek, 1910–12), with 353,533 as an advocacy figure. For the six metropolises of the Pontus only the Greek count exists, 400,586; there is no Ottoman figure for the Pontus as a whole to set against it. The Greek counts covered every Orthodox Christian, Greek- or Turkish-speaking. The Greek memorandum of 1919 claimed 700,000.\n\n## The numbers, and who gives them\n\n| What | Figure | Who gives it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Emigrants with the Russians, 1829–30 | about 42,000, from \"Eastern Anatolia (Erzerum)\" | Hassiotis 1997 |\n| Pontic emigrants to the Caucasus, 1856–82 | about 53,000 | Hassiotis 1997 |\n| Greek speakers in Russian Kars province, 1897 | 32,593 | Russian census |\n| The Kromni list of 1857 | 11,151 houses, 3,452 of them crypto-Christian; an estimated 17,260 crypto-Christians | the Kromlides' list, in Bryer 1983 |\n| Crypto-Christians recognised | about 8,000; about 20,000; 24,000 | Kallifron; Ioannidis; Agtzidis |\n| Circassian refugees, 1864 | about 40,000 at Trabzon; 70,000–80,000 at Samsun | British consular and sanitary reports |\n| Armenians killed in Trebizond, 8 October 1895 | several hundred (400–900 in contemporary reports); 1,000–2,000 in the province | contemporary reports; a modern estimate |\n| Greek schools of the Pontus, about 1910 | about 1,047 | Koromila |\n\n## Where historians disagree\n\nWho the crypto-Christians were, and why they came out, is the chief debate of the century. Bryer saw miners protecting their privileges; Tzedopoulos sees several negotiated identities and a modern political act in 1857; Greek national writing sees secret martyrs of the faith; and Turkish scholarship of the state institutions reads the declarations as the work of foreign powers (see the last section). How many Greeks lived in the Pontus before 1914 is the other: the counts in the table above were each made for a purpose, and neither side's is neutral.\n\n## Legend and evidence\n\nThe evidence for this century is unusually rich: the Kromlides' list of 1857, the consular reports of Stevens, Palgrave and Biliotti, the Russian census of 1897, the Greek census of 1910–12, the church's own counts and the Ottoman census of 1914, with its known undercount of Christians. The legends belong to the derebey wars and the crypto-Christians, whose own stories of their origins, such as Santa's pasture, are told in the previous chapter.\n\n## The sources\n\nBryer's study of the crypto-Christians and Consul Palgrave (1983) is fundamental and based on consular archives; the list of 1857 is on p. 41. Tzedopoulos (2009) is excellent and critical of both national narratives. Andreades (1995) gives useful testimony and lists, from a committed Greek viewpoint. Türkyılmaz (2009) is based on the Ottoman archives and nuanced. Karabıçak (2021) is solid work from the Ottoman archives on the mines and villages. Hassiotis (1997) gives the standard Greek figures for the emigration; they are estimates, and his regional labels are quoted as printed. Rosser-Owen (2007), with the British Foreign Office files and *The Times*, documents 1864. Cuinet (1892) is a French survey from Ottoman data. Karpat (1985) publishes the Ottoman census, in which Christians are undercounted; Kitromilides & Alexandris (1984–85) tabulate the Greek census of 1910–12; Soteriades (1918) carries figures prepared for the peace conference. For 1895 the American diplomatic papers (*Foreign Relations of the United States*), the French consul Cillière's report and the press cables stand against the Ottoman report, and have to be read together. Chrysanthos (1933) gives the church's record of these years.\n\n## What the Turkish sources say\n\n- **Why the crypto-Christians came out.** Kuş (2018, in the Turkish Historical Society's journal *Belleten*) and the study \"Trabzon vilayetinde iki din taşıyanlar\" present the Kromlides and Stavriotes who declared themselves Christian after 1856 as people encouraged by foreign powers, above all Russia, and moved by the wish to escape conscription and gain privileges. *Partly supported:* Ioannidis (1870) says that a secretary of the Russian consulate helped draft the petition of 1857, Palgrave blamed Russian influence, and Bryer agrees that worldly motives and Russian backing played a part, and that the loss of the miners' privileges shaped the timing. Neither supports the charge of insincerity: Tzedopoulos sees several identities and a modern political act, and Deringil sees sovereignty, not religion, as what was at issue.\n- **Trebizond, October 1895.** The report of the officials and notables of Trebizond, forwarded to Washington by Mavroyeni Bey on 27 December 1895, blames the violence on Armenian gunfire, gives no Armenian death toll, and says that \"the Greeks … had joined the brawl\". *Against:* contemporary press and consular reports describe a massacre of Armenians, with several hundred dead in the city. Western accounts say the Greeks were not attacked, and no other source says they took part. The wounding of Bahri Pasha and Hamdi Pasha by two Armenians on 2 October is a fact, and it is told above.\n\nOttoman state records, the registers, orders, yearbooks and the census of 1914, are used in the narrative with their known limits.\n"};
